WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.140
Microsoft.PowerShell.SecretManagement provides a common way for scripts to work with secrets without hard-coding storage logic into every script.

2
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.880
Its value is the vault abstraction.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:24.700
A script can ask for a secret by name through a consistent interface, while the actual backend may be a local store, an enterprise vault, or a cloud secret service through an extension.

4
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.040
This separates script logic from the details of secret storage.

5
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.680
The SecretStore extension vault is commonly used for local scenarios and development workflows.

6
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:42.700
At a high level, a vault is registered, configured, and then used to set or retrieve named secrets.

7
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:50.320
A script can retrieve a credential, token, or other secret at runtime instead of storing the plaintext value inside the script.

8
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:56.740
This reduces credential sprawl and makes the script easier to move between environments where the vault binding may differ.

9
00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.100
The operational model matters.

10
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.800
Registering a vault is not the same as designing access control.

11
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:12.980
Teams still need to decide who can read or write secrets, whether unlock steps are interactive or unattended, how vault access is audited, and how secrets are named.

12
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:21.880
Scripts should request only the secrets they need and should handle missing or unavailable secrets clearly rather than falling back to insecure defaults.

13
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.600
SecretManagement is most useful when it becomes a standard pattern.

14
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:34.660
Instead of each team inventing its own password file, environment variable convention, or local export, scripts can rely on a supported retrieval flow.

15
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:41.720
That makes review easier: the script contains the secret name and the expected use, not the secret value.

16
00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.700
The vault and platform own storage, protection, and policy.

17
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:55.120
Review confirms that the script contains only the secret name and expected use while the vault owns storage, protection, and policy.
